# Pipeline and rubric

## Closed taxonomy

- All flag and credit categories live in `extension/src/pipeline/rubric/categories.js`. That file is the single source of truth.
- The on-device model is only allowed to emit `category` ids from that file. Any new clause class requires a new entry in `categories.js` first; the AI prompt then enumerates it.
- Never let AI invent free-form labels, free-form titles, or free-form summaries. Titles come from `resolveTitle()`; summaries come from `buildSummary()`.

## Code scores, AI classifies

- The 0..100 score is computed by `computeScore(flags, credits)` in `rubric/score.js`. The AI never returns a score.
- The grade letter (A..F) is computed by `gradeOf(score)` in the same file.
- If you add a new category, update its `kind`, `weight`, and severity multipliers in `categories.js` and add a rubric test in `tests/rubric.test.js`.

## Classifier-Verifier pattern

- Stage A (`analyze.js`) is high recall. It emits candidate flags/credits with plain-English category descriptions and strictly negative disambiguation rules in the prompt.
- Stage B (`verify.js`) is high precision. It groups candidates by category and asks the model, one category at a time, whether each candidate matches curated match / not-match examples in `pipeline/rubric/reference-examples.js`.
- Adding a new category requires an entry in `categories.js` AND a `reference-examples.js` block with at least three `match` and three `notMatch` quotes.
- Do not reintroduce keyword-regex guards for classification. The verifier plus reference examples is the extension point.
- The only guard retained in `category-guards.js` is `quoteLooksSpliced`, and it is a hallucination defence, not a classification rule.

## English-only for MVP

- `detect-lang.js` halts the pipeline with `unsupportedLanguage: true` for any non-English document. Do not silently fall back to English extraction on a non-English document.
- The keyword-scoring extractor in `extract.js` assumes English input.

## Quote integrity

- AI must return `quote` as a verbatim substring of the document text. `parseAndValidate()` in `analyze.js` rejects anything else via `quoteAppearsInDocument()`, which uses only strict `docNormalised.includes(probe)` after normalisation. Do not reintroduce head-plus-tail fallbacks.
- Highlighting in `content.js` relies on `normalizeChars()` to fold smart quotes, dashes and NBSP. Never compare raw strings.

## Determinism

- `LanguageModel.create` in `analyze.js` and `verify.js` sets `temperature: 0` and `topK: 1`.
- `session.prompt` uses a JSON-Schema `responseConstraint` that pins service type, category and severity to their closed enums.
- Do not introduce a `Math.random()` or a wall-clock reading anywhere in the scoring path. Deterministic same-in / same-out is a hard requirement.

## Pipeline is a chain of pure steps

- Each step in `pipeline/steps/*.js` takes `(input, ctx)` and returns `{ value }` or `{ value, done: true }` to halt. No global state.
- Add new steps to the `PIPELINE` array in `background.js`. Do not branch step order based on flags inside a step.
- Reuse expensive resources across steps by binding them to `ctx`; never store them at module scope.

## Sensitive-token strip

- Any AI-controlled string surfaced to the user (`verifierReason`, `note`, ...) must be passed through `strip-sensitive-tokens.js` before being persisted. The domain from `input.domain` seeds the token list. Quotes are exempt because they are verbatim by definition.
